      "preview_head": "pulse of a key measure of our water planet: how freshwater cycles over Earth's land surfaces in the form of soil moisture. The mission will produce the most accurate, highest-resolution global maps ev",
      "preview_tail": " covers much of the ground. The mission is expected to operate for at least three years, during which it will continuously monitor changes in global water distribution. NASA\u2019s Jet Propulsion Laborator",
      "full_text": "pulse of a key measure of our water planet: how freshwater cycles over Earth's land surfaces in the form of soil moisture. The mission will produce the most accurate, highest-resolution global maps ever obtained from space of the moisture present in the top layer of soils across the globe. These detailed measurements are crucial for understanding and predicting weather patterns, as well as assessing drought conditions and managing agricultural resources more effectively. By providing data on soil moisture levels with unprecedented accuracy, SMAP will help improve climate models and contribute to better decision-making in areas such as water resource management, flood forecasting, and crop yield predictions. The satellite combines both active radar (which sends signals that bounce off the Earth's surface) and passive radiometry techniques (measuring natural microwave emissions from soil). This dual approach allows SMAP to gather data even under cloudy conditions or when vegetation covers much of the ground. The mission is expected to operate for at least three years, during which it will continuously monitor changes in global water distribution. NASA\u2019s Jet Propulsion Laborator",
      "avg_prediction": 0.5564,
